---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { contact, person } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="About — Sean Denton | Sometimes Creek Ventures"
  description="Sean Denton: 15+ years designing and building scalable cloud architectures. Founder of Sometimes Creek Ventures LLC, Georgetown, TX."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 pt-14 sm:px-8 sm:pt-20">
    <h1
      class="font-display leading-[0.92] font-extrabold tracking-tight uppercase"
      style="font-size: clamp(3rem, 10vw, 7rem);"
    >
      About<span class="text-accent">.</span>
    </h1>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-[16rem_1fr] md:gap-14">
      <div>
        <!-- TODO(sean): replace placeholder with a real photo (square-ish, ~640px). -->
        <div
          class="relative aspect-square border-4 border-ink bg-ink/5"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Photo of Sean Denton coming soon"
        >
          <span
            class="absolute right-3 bottom-3 block h-6 w-6 bg-accent"
            aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>
        <dl class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-sm">
          <div>
            <dt class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-fog uppercase">
              Based in
            </dt>
            <dd class="mt-1 font-medium">{person.location}</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-fog uppercase">
              Education
            </dt>
            <dd class="mt-1 font-medium">{person.education}</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-fog uppercase">
              Patent
            </dt>
            <dd class="mt-1 font-medium">{person.patent}</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-fog uppercase">
              Elsewhere
            </dt>
            <dd class="mt-1 flex gap-5">
              <a
                href={contact.github}
                class="font-bold text-ink underline decoration-accent decoration-2 underline-offset-3 hover:text-accent-deep"
              >
                GitHub
              </a>
              <a
                href={contact.linkedin}
                class="font-bold text-ink underline decoration-accent decoration-2 underline-offset-3 hover:text-accent-deep"
              >
                LinkedIn
              </a>
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-scv">
        <p>
          I'm Sean Denton, a cloud and AI solutions architect with 15+ years
          designing and building scalable cloud architectures. I run every
          engagement through Sometimes Creek Ventures LLC, my consulting
          company, from Georgetown, Texas — remote-first, working with teams
          wherever they are.
        </p>
        <p>
          I studied petroleum engineering at The University of Texas at Austin
          and hold a patent on browser-based content protection (US 8,887,290
          B1). The through-line of the work since: regulated, data-intensive
          platforms — healthcare, aerospace, transportation, retail — where the
          architecture, the security model, and the operational reality all
          have to hold up at once.
        </p>
        <h2>How I work</h2>
        <p>
          I take on the parts of a build that are hard to delegate: the
          architecture, the data and security model, the infrastructure, and
          the path to production. Then I stay accountable through deployment
          and operation — the system running is the deliverable, not the
          design document. That works equally well solo, as it did building
          Cardio's monitoring platform end to end, or anchoring a larger team,
          as it does leading a 15+ engineer squad at CVS Health.
        </p>
        <p>
          The name? A sometimes creek is a Texas Hill Country stream that runs
          dry most of the year — then runs strong when the rain comes. It's an
          honest description of good contract engineering: not always on your
          payroll, fully there when you need it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
